  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 243 PM CDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nCurrently, a strong mid/upper level ridge remains centered just off \nto our south and east. An area of 850mb frontogenesis extends across \ncentral Kansas and led to morning showers for portions of Barton and \nRussell counties. With diurnal heating and this existing weak \nboundary, a few isolated showers/thunderstorms may be possible, \nthough kept POPs low through the afternoon due to very high \nuncertainty. A surface trough remains draped across the Central High \nPlains through the next couple of days, leading to scattered \nrain/storm chances for locations well west of the forecast \narea. Otherwise, broad ridging will dominate the forecast period\nleading to dry weather and above normal temperatures. With the \nridge axis just to our east through the end of the work-week, \nhighs in the upper 90s to around 100 degrees are expected. Mid \nrange guidance indicates the ridge will build back west for the \nweekend into early next week leading to increasing heights and \nwarmer temperatures. Forecast highs for the end of the period \nare not entirely reflective of this persistent ridging pattern \nas NBM guidance is more heavily weighted on climatology later in\nthe period. Regardless, the story remains the same, well above \nnormal temperatures and dry conditions will persist.",
